A||||14||overlord||engine overheating!!!   help||||4
Z||000000||overlord||09-17-1999||12:50 PM||||my 2.5L 4 cyl. is overheating.<P>have changed thermostat and that didnt work. checked the water pump and looks fine...no hoses are collapsed or anything so why is it overheating like this??<P>it was normal one day and the next was overheating..<P>one more thing...when it is overheated i get no heat from vents.   please help..<P><BR>thnx,||||reg||4
Z||000001||Patrick||09-17-1999||12:55 PM||||<BR>That last point of yours is probably important. I'm not too familiar with "burping" the Fiero's cooling system, but it sounds to me like you've got air trapped in the system and the coolant is not circulating properly.<P>||||reg||4
Z||000002||overlord||09-17-1999||01:14 PM||||hmmm...that might be it...<P>cause i noticed when the engine is hot enough to fry eggs on.....the coolant in the radiator is cool, almost like it never got to the engine...<P>so what should i do?, drain the fluid? and anything else???<P><BR>thanx.||||reg||7
Z||000003||theogre||09-17-1999||01:49 PM||||definatly sounds like low coolant or improper filling.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||09-17-1999||02:18 PM||||From the top of my head, this is what you do:<P><OL TYPE=A><BR><LI> Remove cap and thermostat at engine (when cold!)<BR><LI> Remove cap at radiator<BR><LI> Add coolant at back until it overflows in front<BR><LI> Put front cap back on<BR><LI> Put rear cap back on but do not tighten<BR><LI> Start engine and idle for about a minute<BR><LI> Stop engine<BR><LI> Repeat steps B-G<BR><LI> Add coolant in back until it overflows<BR><LI> Put both caps back on (and thermostat)<BR></OL><P>That should get rid of most of the air.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cliff Pennock (edited 09-17-1999).]||||reg||3
Z||000005||batboy||09-17-1999||09:58 PM||||Sometimes it's a bitch getting all the air out of the system. Use Cliff's method except run it for more than a minute. Let it get up to near normal operating temperature, then let it cool back down before adding more water. If it was working ok previously, something is wrong, possibly a leak somewhere. Once you get the air burped out and it's running normal again, carefully and thoroughly check for leaks. Leaks can occur up front at the radiator, anywhere in the tubes that run underneath the car, and in any one of the several hoses (including heater hoses) in the engine compartment area.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Speadraser||09-20-1999||10:57 AM||||Be sure all your hoses are good.  When I bought my 86 SE four years ago the previous owner had a overheating problem.  I bought it with this problem until I observed how my coolant level was getting low.  The overflow recovery tank hose had a small hole in it near the filler neck.  When ever the car would reach operating temps the recovery tank would catch most of the fluid.  But when the system would cool down it would pull air into the system instead of the water.  The previous owner had replaced the water pump and the radiator.  I snipped the hose and bought a .35 cent clamp and it fixed it.  I hope your problem is as easy to fix.<BR>Speady||||reg||12
Z||000007||Dan||09-21-1999||08:26 PM||||Make sure your overflow recovery tank is full at all times,especially when filling.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-21-1999||09:17 PM||||is it still over heating?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bruce||09-22-1999||08:45 PM||||Ogre,<BR>I'm even more confused than before- what am I looking for?<BR>Thanks,<BR>b||||reg||1
Z||000010||K9||09-22-1999||09:07 PM||||After removeing all or most of the air from the system, check to make shure that the inside of your raditor is not blocked. With the car running at normal temp.run your finger along the fins of the rad.All passage way should feel the same temp.If one is cooler then the other, meens that that passage way might be blocked.Good Luck.<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000011||Bruce||09-22-1999||09:30 PM||||My cooling system is fine- just wanted to know the correct way to add coolant, periodically.||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||09-22-1999||09:45 PM||||To just top off the radiator when it's cold, remove the radiator cap and the thermostat cap. Add coolant to the thermostat housing until coolant flows out the radiator. Tighten both caps, add coolant to the overflow tank until it's about half full. Done. This is not burping the cooling system, which is another totally different procedure.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Bruce||09-22-1999||11:04 PM||||Got it.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Pat||09-23-1999||02:30 AM||||I find that by filling the cooling system from the rear hose (by the thermostat cap) as the best way to fill the entire system.<BR>that way the water pump has water to push through the radiator. <P>Can anyone believe that I am currently using a radiator out of a 1980 Ford Fiesta. Some or all Fiero radiators are made out of plastic. This can cause a split and pressure leak...leading to overheating.<BR>There is a plastics kit to repair this. It's about $10. You can find this at Pepboys.||||reg||1
